For selection screens, the link should occupy the bottom-right of the information area immediately above the existing Confirm button.

For Discover, which has no Confirm button, position the link at the bottom-right of its preview information section.

Use Flexbox or CSS Grid so the alignment is reliable. Avoid fragile absolute positioning that might overlap other content.

The result must look intentional on both desktop and mobile.

6. Make the play-style dialog available in all rosters

Currently, Discover uses FighterBrowser and playStyleDialog() to display fighter descriptions through app.infoDialog.

Extend this capability to every roster.

Expected behavior

When a player activates “Read play style”:

1. Open the existing informational modal.
2. Show the currently previewed fighter’s display name.
3. Show the localized play-style description from fighter-profiles.js.
4. Allow the user to read the full description.
5. Close through the existing Close button, Escape, gamepad Back, or backdrop dismissal.
6. Return focus to the same “Read play style” button after closing.

The modal must NOT:

* Confirm the fighter.
* Begin a battle.
* Change the fighter selection.
* Advance setup steps.
* Unpause Practice Ground.
* Trigger any gameplay action.

This is purely an informational interaction.

Preserve existing modal accessibility, navigation scopes, focus trapping, and background inertness.

7. Refactor shared code properly

Avoid independently duplicating the difficulty and description UI across screens.

Recommended approach:

* Move or extract reusable difficulty-rating rendering from FighterBrowser into shared roster functionality.
* Share the “Read play style” control and its profile lookup.
* Make FighterRoster display the profile of the currently previewed fighter.
* Keep FighterBrowser’s Discover-specific read-only behavior.
* Preserve each roster’s independent state, unique preview IDs, navigation, and confirmation callbacks.
* Reuse the existing InfoDialog and localized fighter descriptions.
* Avoid circular imports when reorganizing playStyleDialog().

The implementation must remain character-agnostic.

Do not hardcode fighter IDs, names, star counts, or descriptions into UI components.

Every future playable fighter with a valid profile should automatically receive the same UI.

8. Handle locked and missing-profile fighters

Maintain current roster behavior.

Playable fighter with a valid profile:

* Show five difficulty stars.
* Show “Read play style”.
* Open the corresponding description.

Playable fighter without a profile:

* Show the existing “Not rated” fallback.
* Hide the play-style action.
* Do not crash or show stale information.

Locked or disabled fighter:

* Retain its Locked state.
* Hide difficulty stars.
* Hide “Read play style”.
* Do not load or reveal character details.

No playable fighters:

* Preserve existing empty-roster behavior.
* Keep Confirm disabled.
* Do not show a misleading difficulty rating or description.

9. Responsive design

Ensure the new layout works across:

* Desktop screens
* Compact landscape windows
* Mobile landscape
* Tablets
* Short-height viewports
* Practice Ground’s roster overlays
* Discover’s responsive layouts

Requirements:

* Stars must remain clearly visible.
* The smaller action must remain legible and clickable.
* “Read play style” should stay right-aligned.
* Text must not overlap the fighter’s name or Confirm button.
* Avoid clipping and horizontal overflow.
* Preserve a useful amount of space for the animated fighter preview.
* Do not unnecessarily enlarge the bottom information panel.
* French translations must fit or wrap gracefully.

Use existing CSS variables and conventions rather than introducing unrelated styling.

10. Preserve existing gameplay and navigation

Do not alter:

* Fighter abilities or combat mechanics.
* Character availability.
* The 48-slot roster structure.
* Portraits and preview animations.
* Character selection and confirmation logic.
* CPU selection.
* Quick Battle or Watch Mode setup progression.
* Practice Ground fighter swapping.
* Existing keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad navigation.
* Existing character profile ratings or description content.

Discover must remain read-only, while regular selection screens must retain their existing Confirm buttons and behavior.

